    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- Header -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pt-32 pb-12 md:pt-44 md:pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <span class="font-mono text-[10px] text-scale uppercase tracking-[0.2em] block mb-6">Essay</span>
      <h1 class="font-display italic text-bone leading-[1.05] text-[2.5rem] md:text-[3.5rem] lg:text-[4.5rem]">
        Trust the Fucking Agent
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-mist mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Most agent setups today fail at the same thing: the human asks the agent what to do, then does it themselves. That isn't oversight. That's bad delegation in a model costume.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-mist mt-4">Ari Mendelow &middot; May 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Body -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pb-20 md:pb-28">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-ouro">

<p>The moment keeps happening in different forms.</p>

<p>You build an agent. You give it tools. You wire up identity, tasks, shell access, maybe a coding lane. You tell people it can really work. Then the moment something matters, you step in front of it. You copy the output into the right file yourself. You translate the bug report into "something clearer." You approve the plan, trim the prompt, run the command, inspect the diff, and hand it back like room service. The agent is technically in the loop. You're still doing the job.</p>

<p>The story you tell yourself in the moment sounds reasonable. It's faster if I just do it. I know what I want. Re-explaining costs more than executing. None of these are lies, exactly. They are the small, plausible sentences delegation dies inside of.</p>

<p>The table just flipped. You hired an agent and turned yourself into its agent. It has the brain job — analyzing, planning, deciding. You have the hands job — typing, pasting, ferrying outputs between windows. That isn't a workflow. That's a costume change.</p>

<p>The pattern shows up at every scale. The agent drafts a migration plan and you implement it. The agent diagnoses the issue and you write the patch. The agent surfaces three options and you pick one and execute it manually because you didn't fully trust the third. Each step looks defensible in isolation. Stacked together, you have rebuilt the workflow you supposedly automated, with extra steps.</p>

<p>It's operator clinginess wearing a model costume. Permission is necessary; the failure is making the human restate the same authority over and over, in chat, every turn.</p>

<h2>You'd lose it</h2>

<p>Delegation is a learned skill. Most people are bad at it, and the agent is the first thing in years that's been honest with you about how bad.</p>

<p>Picture this. Someone asks you to send a calendar invite. They then stand behind your chair and narrate it. Open Outlook. No, the other Outlook. Click the calendar icon. Click New Event. Type the subject — capitalize Quarterly — tab to the time field, no, not that one. You didn't ask. You've sent a thousand calendar invites. You'd lose it. The patronizing part isn't even the inefficiency; it's the assumption that you couldn't have figured out the next step on your own.</p>

