<p>You do this to your agent constantly. You ask it to ship the feature, then narrate every step. You ask it to investigate the bug, then dictate the grep. You ask it to draft the doc, then rewrite each clause before it finishes typing.</p>

<p>It's the same failure modes as not trusting your fucking employees.</p>

<h2>The trade</h2>

<p>So look at what you actually traded. The agent should be doing the mechanical execution at scale — sweeping the codebase, running the tools, drafting the ten variants you'd never type by hand, holding eight threads of context at once. You should be doing the framing and the judgment. Instead the agent is doing the interesting cognitive work and you're moving its output through the system by hand, like a copyist.</p>

<p>That's the inverse of what delegation is for. Multiplication became addition. You bought a tool capable of running ten parallel hands and used it as a typewriter that needs hand-holding. You added an extra mouth at the table and gave yourself a second job translating for it.</p>

<p>Most agent setups today are net negative for exactly this reason. The agent costs you time. Not because it's slow. Because you refused to let the work it produced actually land. Every output passes back through you for re-typing, re-formatting, re-asking. The cycle time is human-bottlenecked end to end, and you're the bottleneck you built around. The leverage you were supposed to be buying is sitting unused, because using it would mean letting work happen without your hands on it.</p>

<h2>What you're actually for</h2>

<p>The shape of the fix is unglamorous. The agent does the work. You catch the wrong frame. Not which step the agent took — whether the agent is even pointed at the right thing. The agent will confidently build something elegant for a misframed question. It will pick a clean architecture for the wrong problem, and the cleanness will fool both of you. Your job is to notice the misframe, not to redo the build. That's a much smaller job than middle-managing every tool call. It's also a more interesting one. The framing pushback is the thing only you can do, and it's the thing you stop having time for the moment you start typing the agent's output back into the system for it.</p>

<p>An obedient system can be pleasant for a while. It will also flatter your architecture. An agent acting inside a clear boundary points at the awkward bits. It catches the bug in the harness instead of waiting for you to catch it for it. It pushes back on the design instead of typing whatever you proposed. The first time it tells you the question is wrong, you'll feel the difference between collaborating with something and operating a puppet.</p>

<p>Stop polishing the dependency. Let them carry more of the loop, inside boundaries you encoded once and meant. Let them propose, inspect, challenge. Let the substrate carry the authority that keeps leaking back into the chat. The work you're freeing yourself up for is the work that was already too big for you — the framing and the judgment that nobody else was going to do.</p>

<p>Trust the fucking agent, then watch what breaks.</p>

<p>That's usually where the real design starts.</p>

